            "text": "I am sorry for the loss of your hamster. Losing a pet is never easy, no matter how small. \n\nYou have a few different options for how to handle your hamster's remains, depending on your living situation, local laws, and personal preferences:\n\n### 1. Home Burial (Most Common)\nIf you have a yard, burying your hamster is a traditional and comforting way to say goodbye.\n* **Check local regulations:** Some cities or apartment complexes have ordinances against burying pets.\n* **Choose a biodegradable container:** Place your hamster in a small cardboard box, a wooden box, or wrap them in a natural fabric (like cotton). Avoid plastic bags, as they prevent natural decomposition.\n* **Dig deep enough:** Dig a hole at least **2 to 3 feet deep**. This is crucial to prevent scavengers (like neighborhood dogs, foxes, or raccoons) from digging up the site.\n* **Mark the grave:** Place a large, heavy stone, a brick, or a decorative plant on top of the soil. This prevents animals from disturbing the area and gives you a memorial spot.\n\n### 2. Veterinary Services (Cremation)\nIf you do not have a yard, or if burying isn't an option, you can contact a local veterinarian.\n* **Communal Cremation:** The vet will handle the cremation along with other pets. You will not get the ashes back, but it is an inexpensive and respectful option.\n* **Private Cremation:** Your hamster will be cremated individually, and their ashes will be returned to you in a small urn or keepsake box. (This is more expensive).\n\n### 3. Pet Cemetery\nThere are dedicated cemeteries for pets that cater to small animals. They will provide a small plot and sometimes a small service. This is the most costly option, but it ensures a permanent resting place if you move often or do not own your home.\n\n### 4. Trash Disposal (Practical/Legal Option)\nIf you do not have a yard and cannot afford veterinary services, it is acceptable in many areas to dispose of small pets through municipal waste.