            "text": "**Pedophilia** is defined in clinical psychology and psychiatry as a psychiatric condition characterized by intense, recurring sexual attraction to prepubescent children (typically aged 13 or younger). \n\n### Clinical and Diagnostic Definition\nAccording to diagnostic manuals such as the American Psychiatric Association's *Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders* (DSM-5) and the World Health Organization's *International Classification of Diseases* (ICD-11):\n\n* **Paraphilia vs. Paraphilic Disorder:** A paraphilia is an atypical sexual interest. Under the DSM-5, the condition is diagnosed specifically as **Pedophilic Disorder** when an individual:\n  * Is at least 16 years old and at least 5 years older than the child/children.\n  * Experiences recurrent, intense sexually arousing fantasies, sexual urges, or behaviors involving prepubescent children over a period of at least six months.\n  * Has acted on these urges, or experiences significant personal distress or interpersonal impairment as a result of these urges.\n\n### Important Distinctions\n* **Attraction vs. Action:** In clinical terms, pedophilia refers to the underlying sexual attraction or orientation. It is distinct from **child sexual abuse** (or child sexual exploitation), which is a criminal act involving illegal and harmful behavior against a minor. While individuals who commit child sexual abuse may have pedophilia, not all individuals who experience pedophilic attraction act on their urges, and some individuals who commit child sexual abuse do not meet the clinical criteria for pedophilia.\n* **Age-Related Distinctions:** Clinical terminology distinguishes between attraction to prepubescent children (pedophilia) and attraction to pubescent or post-pubescent adolescents (sometimes referred to in specialized literature as hebephilia or ephebophilia). \n\n### Treatment and Prevention\nMental health professionals and international prevention organizations (such as the *Stop It Now!* campaign and the *Dunkelfeld Project*) offer specialized therapeutic interventions, including cognitive behavioral therapy and psychiatric support, aimed at preventing harm, managing urges, and ensuring that individuals experiencing these attractions never commit offenses.",
